  "narrative": {
    "northStar": "The Shade Card",
    "overview": "A textile dye-house shade card: an indigo-dyed shell holds a pale card mount, and each machine is a swatch window cut into it. Undyed swatches are healthy; only trouble takes colour. The worst machine is mounted first and biggest. The system serves two readers who never share a screen: the maintenance head at a laptop or a wall TV in the control room, and the owner on a mid-range Android phone opening a push alert. Both must know which machine is in trouble in under a second, from across a room, without reading colour.\n\nDensity is operational, not decorative. Surfaces are flat panes separated by 1px hairline rules (the fleet grid draws its rules with a 1px gap over a `rule`-coloured ground), not by floating cards. The machine itself is the interface: every tile carries a live, drag-to-rotate 3D twin whose breaching part glows, and status is a word first, then an icon, a pattern and a colour.\n\nWeb (`frontend/src/index.css`) and Android (`ui/theme/Color.kt`) share the same hex tokens and the same face, Archivo. The status verdict is never computed in a client: `backend/health.py` returns `danger | check | ok | no_signal`, and both clients only draw it.",
    "keyCharacteristics": [
      "Indigo shell (top bar, ribbon, Floor view ground), pale card mount, white and tile panes.",
      "Status = word + icon + pattern + colour, never colour alone.",
      "Worst-first ordering everywhere: ribbon, fleet grid, Floor view.",
      "Square corners on content; 4px only on controls.",
      "Archivo throughout, tabular figures on by default.",
      "One live 3D twin per machine, glowing only on the breaching part."
    ],
    "rules": [
      {
        "name": "The Only-Trouble-Is-Dyed Rule",
        "body": "Red, amber, green and grey are status, nothing else. No decorative use, no brand accent in a status hue; chrome stays indigo, ink and paper.",
        "section": "colors"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Never-Colour-Alone Rule",
        "body": "Every status mark carries the word (DANGER, CHECK, OK, NO SIGNAL), its icon (bar-in-disc, warning triangle, tick, cross) and its pattern (solid, hatch, outline, dots). The ribbon chip on Android is the one place the word is dropped for width; icon and pattern remain.",
        "section": "colors"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Backend-Owns-Status Rule",
        "body": "Clients never derive status from raw readings. `backend/health.py` is the single source; `RANK` (danger, check, no_signal, ok) only orders it.",
        "section": "colors"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Across-The-Room Rule",
        "body": "The status word is the largest type in its container. The worst machine's chip runs full-width at xl or xxl.",
        "section": "typography"
      },
      {
        "name": "The No-Soft-Card Rule",
        "body": "Tiles, feed, side panel and chips never take a drop shadow. If it sits in the grid, it is flat.",
        "section": "elevation"
      }
    ],
    "dos": [
      "**Do** take every status from `backend/health.py` and draw it with `StatusChip`; add the word, icon and pattern, not just a colour.",
      "**Do** order machines worst first (danger, check, no_signal, ok) in every list, ribbon and grid.",
      "**Do** use the shared hex tokens; a new colour goes into both `index.css` and `Color.kt`.",
      "**Do** separate panes with 1px rules over a Hairline ground.",
      "**Do** follow the `part_` / `pin_` / `spin_` / `run` naming contract for any new machine model.",
      "**Do** render new web twins through the shared scissored canvas in `stage.js`."
    ],
    "donts": [
      "**Don't** use red, amber, green or grey for anything that is not machine status.",
      "**Don't** fill with flat amber or flat green; CHECK is hatched, OK is outlined.",
      "**Don't** put drop shadows on tiles, panels or chips; shadows are for floating layers only.",
      "**Don't** round content corners; 4px is for controls.",
      "**Don't** compute or override status in a client.",
      "**Don't** present simulated data without its SIMULATED tag."
    ]
  }
}